  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Bangladesh Emergency  Response and  Recovery Programme</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">In consideration of the magnitude of the flood and damages caused in the flood affected areas, this  response will contribute to prevent the affected people to adopt the negative coping mechanisms.  While all populations are at risk of waterborne diseases, women and children are most vulnerable as  they have limited movement outside house to get access to the water and sanitation. The prevailing  health environment makes children especially prone to waterborne diseases as they have less  immunity than adults. Without the immediate provision of safe drinking water and basic sanitation  support, outbreaks of Diarrhoea and other waterborne diseases may cause more health hazards for  the affected communities.   Safe drinking water, sanitation, hygiene promotion and food have become prime needs in the  affected areas. Oxfam is conducting a rapid sectoral needs assessment in the affected areas. The  experience with Multi -Purpose Cash Grant (MPG) shows that immediate unrestricted and  unconditional cash support helps affected people to ensure their immediate food and other basic  needs. Oxfam has presence in the areas with contingency stocks, where we are working through  solid and reliable local partners.</narrative>
  </description>
